  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/c/c1/Avengers_Vol_1_2.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Hulk, Thor and Iron Man await the arrival of Ant-Man and the Wasp at their next Avengers meeting, which is to take place in the \"library of Anthony Stark, millionaire weapons manufacturer.\"&nbsp; Once Hank and Jan appear (continuing their blatant pill popping from the last issue of <i>Tales to Astonish</i> -- <i>yeesh</i>), they sit down to, as Iron Man points out, \"get to know each other better.\"\n\nMeanwhile we see the Space Phantom arriving on Earth, shaped as a \"strange egg-like object\" where he plainly indicates it's been \"weeks of faster-than-light travel.\"&nbsp; He lands on Earth and then <i>immediately</i> sets out to \"find the Avengers and begin Phase Two.\"&nbsp; He's arrived on Earth to destroy the Avengers so that \"no power on Earth can stop the total invasion by my people!\"\n\nWe learn the Space Phantom can take on the form of anyone, while at the same time banishing that person to \"limbo\" until such time as he assumes <i>another</i> form.&nbsp; Because he's been observing the Avengers \"for months from space,\" he knows they're meeting in Tony Stark's mansion.&nbsp; And even though he's detected intruding, the first person to confront him is the Hulk, whose form he immediately takes, which allows him to stir up a brawl between the Avengers, all to stir up trouble in their ranks.\n\nLater, while out on the street, he is approached by Rick Jones, who obviously knows him.&nbsp; Through Rick, the Space Phantom learns that the Hulk has another identity which he apparently did not know.&nbsp; He also learns that the Hulk travels from place to place by taking tremendous leaps.&nbsp; After he reveals his power to Rick Jones, he uses the Hulk's powerful legs to carry him toward back toward the Avengers.\n\nOn the way, however, he passes by one of Stark International's facilities, where they are about to test an \"anti-missile gun.\"&nbsp; He decides to destroy it to cause more mayhem that the Hulk will ultimately be blamed for, and as a result, Iron Man soon flies out to confront him.&nbsp; During the ensuing battle, the Space Phantom assumes another shape, leaving Iron Man now facing the <i>real</i> Hulk, but being completely unaware of the change.\n\nMeanwhile, Rick Jones has somehow already \"reached the nearest member of his nation-wide Teen Brigade\" and sends out an urgent warning to Giant-Man.&nbsp; Now alerted to the conflict, it doesn't take long for Giant-Man and the Wasp to join in the fray between Iron Man and the Hulk.\n\n\n\n\nWhen Giant-Man and the Wasp join the fray, Iron Man calls him \"Giant-Man\" - so somehow he too has become aware of [LINK:1963:TTA_49_giant0|TTA #49].&nbsp; In the last panel of page 14, the Hulk calls Iron Man \"shell-head.\"&nbsp; This is the first time I remember reading that particular nickname for Iron Man.&nbsp; It's possible that this is its origin point.\n\nWhen Space Phantom assumes the form of Giant-Man, literally as he's disappearing, Hank says \"I'm whirling into another dimension...a shadowy, silent world!\"&nbsp; Wow, he sure can process info fast - we can still see the bottom half of his body in the panel!\n\nMore pages of fighting follow, and Jan runs to Dr. Don Blake, whom she's been told can summon Thor.&nbsp; He sends her out of his office, stamps his cane on the ground to become Thor and <i>immediately</i> calls her back into the office, with Thor there instead of Blake - <i>seriously?</i>  How dumb does he think she is?&nbsp; After filling him in, Thor is already calling out \"Death to the Space Phantom!\"&nbsp; <i>Wow!</i>\n\nThor arrives and deduces that the figure of Iron Man is really the Space Phantom, and calling down torrential rain and lightning, he <i>rusts the Iron Man armor</i> in seconds!&nbsp; (<i>Holy cow!&nbsp; </i>This would seem to be a serious design flaw in the armor suit, and I cannot believe Stark would really be that dumb.)&nbsp; But the Space Phantom then chooses to assume the form of Thor, but he has miscalculated - Thor is not a normal human, so the Space Phantom goes to limbo instead and vanishes.&nbsp; The real Iron Man returns (and un-rusted).\n\nAs the dust settles and they talk about the fighting they did amongst themselves, the Hulk declares that they all hate him deep down and jumps off, quitting the team just like that!&nbsp; What will the world do now without the Avengers to keep the Hulk in check?",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME -- write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME -- write something",
  "review_text": "This was the first time I've dived deep into this issue and realized how pathetic it really is.&nbsp; It was an absolute disaster when it comes to consistency and a dreadful follow-up issue to the solid-enough first one.&nbsp; It was so full of plot holes that if the story itself had been even decent, they would have ruined any enjoyment.\n\nAs I was reluctant to litter the plot summary with a bunch of knee-jerk reactions, I instead will just present a list of [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=\"What the--?\"] moments:\n\n[HANG0] * When Hank and Jan arrive on page 2 (popping pills again), no one ever bothers to ask him what happened to Ant-Man.&nbsp; They all just assume that the costumed man next to the Wasp is him.&nbsp; The <i>cover</i> at least acknowledges that Giant-Man is a new character and points him out to the reader.\n\n[ASSET: right | CreepyAlien | width=15%] [HANG0] * Page 3 indicates that the Space Phantom has been in his egg-shaped spacecraft after \"weeks of faster-than-light travel,\" sent to Earth to \"destroy the Avengers.\"\n[BR]\n[BR] Not only is this yet <i>another</i> alien invasion story, but how in the world has the Space Phantom even <i>heard</i> of the Avengers, who <i>just formed</i>?&nbsp; You'd think that if it's taken him weeks of faster-than-light travel, he'd arrive on Earth with little to no information about current events on Earth, particularly the newly-formed group, even though he says on page 4, panel 4, he's been observing them \"for months from space!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=error; text=(<i>Huh?</i>  They just <i>formed</i>!&nbsp; It's already been <b><i>months</i></b><i>?</i>)]\n[BR]\n[BR] All the same, you do at least have to love those eyeballs that Kirby draws him with.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=What a creepy-looking character -- pointed hair and pointed ears to <i>boot</i>!]\n\n[HANG0] * Page 4 suggests that the Space Phantom knows that Iron Man is Tony Stark because he's been observing them from space.&nbsp; (<i>Boy</i>, just like the Skrulls from [LINK:1963:FF_18|FF #18], this guy must have some <i>truly advanced</i> live television technology!)\n\nThor has no problem calling the Hulk a \"savage\" on page 5.\n[ASSET: center | YouSavage | width=67%]\n\n[HANG0] * Page 7 shows Rick Jones on a New York City street, someone who actually <i>knows</i> the Hulk.&nbsp; But in the previous issue, Rick Jones and the Teen Brigade were in their headquarters, \"in the Southwest, halfway across the country.\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=What's he now doing in New York?]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=Sigh...]\n\n[ASSET: right | DonBlake | width=12%] [HANG0] * Rick can't know the Hulk all <i>that</i> well, because he says that he helps the Hulk \"turn back to [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=Doctor Don Blake].\"\n[BR]\n[BR] (<i>Uh</i>...obviously the Hulk isn't the <i>only</i> one Rick seems to know - Don Blake is <i>Thor's</i> alter-ego!)\n\n[HANG0] * The Space Phantom learns from Rick Jones that the Hulk has another identity, which seems to surprise him.&nbsp; But if he's really been observing the Avengers \"for months,\" how is it that he knows that Iron Man is Tony Stark while at the same time being ignorant that the Hulk is really Bruce Banner?\n[BR]\n[BR] Not only that, but he also seems surprised to learn that the Hulk travels the countryside by leaping from place to place.&nbsp; Wouldn't you have learned that <i>too</i> during your observations?\n\n[HANG0] * Even though the \"Hulk\" jumped he and Rick out to the countryside on page 9 (see the rocks in the background), Rick still manages to make it to the \"nearest member of his nation-wide Teen Brigade\" on page 13, even though page 10 says that the Hulk was only \"a short distance away\" from Stark's missile test, where the fighting broke out between Iron Man and the Hulk.\n[BR]\n[BR] Not only is this <i>amazing</i> timing on Rick's behalf, but take note of <i>huge</i> that ham radio is on the first panel of page 13.&nbsp; That is some <i>serious</i> setup for a teenager!&nbsp; <i>Hahahaha!</i>\n\n[HANG0] * Obviously Rick Jones is more informed on the latest news than the Avengers themselves, for on page 13, he sends out a call to \"Giant-Man\" rather than \"Ant-Man\" on his ham radio, but the splash page had the Avengers wondering where <b>Ant-Man</b> was, as they couldn't start their meeting <i>without</i> him.\n\n[HANG0] * x\n[HANG0] * x\n\n\n\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(Good God!)]\n\nSuffice it to say that consistency departed this magazine almost from its very first page.&nbsp; It is remarkable how an issue filled with such inconsistencies made it to print.\n\n\n\nIt's at least nice to know that, right from the very start, Tony Stark provides the Avengers' funding and headquarters.&nbsp; Not only that, but page 10 of this story comes right out and says that Iron Man is on Stark's payroll.&nbsp; That notion was only <i>just introduced</i> in last month's [LINK:1963:TOS_47_iron0|TOS #47], and already, it seems to be common knowledge.\n\n\nThe only redeeming quality in my mind that prevented this issue from getting an even lower grade was the novelty of having the Hulk quit the group in only the second issue -- something I'm sure nobody saw coming.&nbsp; I don't know if this was the plan all along by Lee and Kirby or if it was something they decided on later in the couple of months of real time between issues 1 and 2 to think about and decide that the Hulk could never really be a good member (not to mention the fact that they already had Thor and Iron Man for brute strength, and now Giant-Man joins too -- the Hulk seems expendable no matter what way you choose to look at it).\n\nBut the gaping inconsistencies in this book completely sabotages it.&nbsp; Just...downright...terrible!&nbsp; Having done some writing of my own, I know how deep into the weeds of a story the author can get -- in this case writing panel-by-panel.&nbsp; You can easily lose track of the larger matter of how little real time has elapsed.&nbsp; And this story just completely forgets the fact that it (the whole story) elapses in seemingly an hour or less.&nbsp; There's simply no way all these other side events could have taken place at the same time!&nbsp; Just no way!\n\nThis is definitely in the top-five (\"bottom\" five?) worst Silver Age Marvels ever, being only slightly less horrible than two others that immediately spring to mind -the Ant-Man story involving the radioactive bug that tries to rule the world ([LINK:1962:TTA_39_ant0|TTA #39]), and Iron Man's bout with that other \"Doctor Strange\" ([LINK:1963:TOS_41_iron0|TOS #41]) -- both of those are indeed even worse.&nbsp; But this one ranks right \"down\" there with those and is by far the one riddled with the most plot holes.&nbsp; It was tough to read this!\n\n[ASSET: left | ThreeToes | width=12%] The Hulk is clearly shown on the splash page as having only three toes on both feet (and there's absolutely no doubt about the count here -- it's quite obvious).&nbsp; I never noticed this before now, so I looked at the previous issue of Avengers to compare.&nbsp; Kirby doesn't provide any close-ups on the feet in that issue, but you can tell he has at least 4 present, and going back even farther, in Incredible [LINK:1962:HULK_5_hulk1|Hulk #5], the opening panel of the second feature story, Kirby draws him with 5 toes, and in #6, Steve Ditko draws at least one panel where you can see 5 toes as well, so while no one really provides a great close-up, in all depictions before this one where you get at least some close-up on the feet, the Hulk is drawn with more than 3, so Jack Kirby seems to have gotten confused here!&nbsp; Not only that, but even in this very issue, Kirby seems to go back and forth between 3 and 4 toes per foot.&nbsp; Don't believe me?&nbsp; Look at the last panel on page 9 and compare to the first one on page 10!&nbsp; It does seem like for the most part, he draws him with 4.&nbsp; But enough of this...!&nbsp; (Except to say that this was called out in Roger Gilman's letter, printed in AVEN #5 as well, to which the response was a call-out to the readership to vote on how many toes \"ol' green skin\" should have.)\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nI'll at least give Kirby some love for the Space Phantom's appearance -- that guy can sure draw memorable villains!\n\nWe see yet another alien invasion plot -- thwarted of course by the Avengers.",
  "side_note": [
    "[ASSET: right | NextIssue | width=12%] Note Iron Man's head on this full-page ad for next issue.&nbsp; (And then look at the cover for next issue and see if that's how he looks.)"
  ],
  "decoder_ring": "[ASSET: left | Shellhead | width=12%] The bottom of page 14 shows the Hulk calling Iron Man \"shell-head\" (apparently its first usage).\n[BR]\n[BR] This is a nickname for Iron Man that becomes so popular that the letters page of his own magazine was eventually called [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Sock It To Shell-Head] for years.",
  "highlight": "FIXME -- write something"
}